  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nThe expansive surface high dominates through the rest of the \nweek and the first half of this coming weekend with a bone dry \ncolumn. This will allow for diurnal mixing to add a degree or \ntwo to afternoon highs and erode a few degrees off of dew \npoints. With the surface high just to the east, a modest \nsoutherly gradient flow will promote warm air advection,\nresulting in warmer temperatures. This is the start of a three \nday stretch with afternoon highs in the 60s for the vast \nmajority of the area. 70 degrees isn't even out of the question \non Saturday south of Pittsburgh where 20%-40% probabilities \nsneak in.\n\nEarly morning river valley fog development is expected once\nagain beneath strong high pressure, cool overnight lows, and \nlight to calm wind. Due to the slightly warmer low temperatures,\nit will likely not be as dense as Tuesday and Wednesday\nmorning. Fog will dissipate after sunrise as temperatures begin\nto rise.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nOur next chance for rain is still on track to arrive late this \nweekend as a trough arrives from the west. Previously mentioned \nuncertainty has begun to clear itself up a bit as we're starting to \nsee a bit better agreement with the two features we've been \nmonitoring the past couple days as they unfold upstream. It does \nappear that we're favoring a less phased solution with a faster \nshortwave sliding southwest through Manitoba, and the southern \nstream energy ejecting out of the Rockies losing its steam with\neastward progression. This helps keep the coastal low moving up\nfrom Florida to the east helping to result in a lower rainfall\nsolution. \n\nWe're seeing that trend in the NBM as the 90th percentile \nwetter end of the goal posts is decreasing and now is only as \nhigh as 0.2-0.3\" for ridges in southwestern PA and northern WV.\nAreas to the west are most likely to see <0.20\".",
